<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Medicaid Work Requirements: {{ state.name }}</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="state_brief.css">
</head>
<body>

<div class="state-name">{{ state.name }}</div>

{# ============ Page 1 — Cover + hero ============ #}
<section class="cover">
  <div class="cover-header">
    <div class="cover-meta">
      OBBBA Section 71119 · §1902(xx)<br>
      Effective December 31, 2026
    </div>
    <div class="logo-block">
      17A<br>
      <small>State &amp; Local AI · Operations Briefing</small>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h1>Medicaid Work Requirements:<br>{{ state.name }}</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Operational briefing for state Medicaid leadership</div>

  {% if state.expansion %}
    <div class="hero-number">{{ state.subject_count_compact }}</div>
    <p class="hero-caption">
      adults in {{ state.name }} are estimated to be subject to the 80-hour-per-month
      work requirement starting <strong>December 31, 2026</strong>. About
      <strong>{% if loss_breakdown %}{{ loss_breakdown.total_loss_compact }}{% else %}{{ state.loss_exposure_compact }}{% endif %}</strong> are projected to lose Medicaid coverage by 2034 —
      most not because they fail to work, but because they can't clear the six-month renewal paperwork.
    </p>

    <div class="stats-strip">
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Subject to verification</div>
        <div class="value">{{ state.subject_count_compact }}</div>
        <div class="sub">{{ state.subject_count_full }} adults</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Projected loss by 2034</div>
        <div class="value">{% if loss_breakdown %}{{ loss_breakdown.total_loss_compact }}{% else %}{{ state.loss_exposure_compact }}{% endif %}</div>
        <div class="sub">{% if loss_breakdown %}{{ loss_breakdown.total_loss_full }}{% else %}{{ state.loss_exposure_full }}{% endif %} people</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="label">Ex parte capability</div>
        <div class="value">{% if loss_breakdown and loss_breakdown.ex_parte_score is not none %}{{ loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.composite if loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores else loss_breakdown.ex_parte_score }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</div>
        <div class="sub">/ 100 composite</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="cover-framing">
      {% if concentration.n50 %}
        Within {{ state.name }}, the burden is not evenly spread. <strong>{{ concentration.n50 }} counties
        ({{ concentration.pct_counties_for_50 }} of all {{ concentration.total_counties }} statewide) hold 50% of the
        affected enrollees</strong>, led by <strong>{{ concentration.top_county_name }}</strong>
        at {{ concentration.top_county_share }} of the state's subject pool.
      {% endif %}
      This brief has five parts: why your losses will look the way they do, where verification will
      fail hardest, how capable your state already is at automatic verification, and what to do before
      the deadline.
    </p>

  {% else %}
    {# Non-expansion state — short cover only #}
    <div class="callout amber">
      <div class="headline">{{ state.name }} has not adopted ACA Medicaid expansion.</div>
      <div>
        The OBBBA work requirement applies specifically to the Medicaid expansion-adult eligibility
        group (Category VIII, ages 19–64, ≤138% FPL), which does not exist here. The working-age
        uninsured population that <em>would</em> be subject if your state expanded is shown
        separately. The verification-system build is moot in your current policy posture.
      </div>
      {% if state.note %}<div style="margin-top:8pt; font-style: italic;">{{ state.note }}</div>{% endif %}
    </div>
    <p class="cover-framing">
      This brief covers the working-age uninsured baseline and peer-state comparisons that may inform
      future expansion-policy decisions.
    </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

{% if state.expansion %}

{# ============ Page 2 — Why your losses look the way they do (sankey lead) ============ #}
{% if loss_breakdown %}
<section class="loss-breakdown">
  <h2>Why your state's losses will look the way they do</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Our bottom-up model projects <strong>{{ loss_breakdown.total_loss_full }}</strong> {{ state.name }} adults will lose
    Medicaid by 2034 under this provision. Most are not losing it because they aren't working. They lose it
    because the verification system can't confirm what's already true about them.{% if loss_breakdown.top_driver %}
    In {{ state.name }}, the single largest at-risk group is <strong>{{ loss_breakdown.top_driver.label }}</strong>
    (about {{ loss_breakdown.top_driver.count_compact }}).{% endif %}
  </p>

  {% if loss_breakdown.sankey_svg %}
    <div class="sankey">{{ loss_breakdown.sankey_svg | safe }}</div>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="loss-summary">
    <div class="loss-bucket loss-bucket--workdoc">
      <div class="bucket-label">Compliant but can't prove it</div>
      <div class="bucket-value">{{ loss_breakdown.workdoc_total_compact }}</div>
      <div class="bucket-share">{{ loss_breakdown.workdoc_pct }} of state loss</div>
    </div>
    <div class="loss-bucket loss-bucket--exemption">
      <div class="bucket-label">Eligible for exemption, can't prove it</div>
      <div class="bucket-value">{{ loss_breakdown.exemption_total_compact }}</div>
      <div class="bucket-share">{{ loss_breakdown.exemption_pct }} of state loss</div>
    </div>
    <div class="loss-bucket loss-bucket--noncompliant">
      <div class="bucket-label">Not in qualifying activity</div>
      <div class="bucket-value">{{ loss_breakdown.noncompliant_compact }}</div>
      <div class="bucket-share">{{ loss_breakdown.noncompliant_pct }} of state loss</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-note">
    Each subgroup is sized bottom-up from ACS PUMS, SAMHSA, BJS, and the academic Section 1115
    work-requirement literature; the full parameter set is in the methodology on the last page. Every
    county, with subject count and projected loss, is in the CSV download.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{# ============ Page 3 — Where verification will fail hardest ============ #}
{% if appendix|length > 0 %}
<section class="geography">
  <h2>Where verification will fail hardest</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Even in capable states, some share of subjects ends up on the paperwork path. The compliance-burden
    index marks where that path is most likely to defeat eligible enrollees. It's a composite of three
    predictors from prior Section 1115 implementations (Arkansas 2018, Georgia Pathways 2023–):
    limited-English households, low broadband access, and concentrated seasonal, gig, and hospitality
    employment. Counties above the national median aren't where people work less; they're where the
    paperwork fails more often.
  </p>

  {% if maps.state_burden %}
    <img class="map" src="{{ maps.state_burden }}" alt="{{ state.name }} compliance-burden index map" />
  {% endif %}

  {% if burden_top5 %}
    <h3>Highest-burden counties in {{ state.name }}</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>County</th>
          <th class="right">Burden index</th>
          <th class="right">Subject enrollees</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for r in burden_top5 %}
        <tr>
          <td class="county-name">{{ r.name }}</td>
          <td class="right">{{ r.burden }}</td>
          <td class="right">{{ "{:,}".format(r.subject) }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{# ============ Page 4 — Ex parte verification capability ============ #}
{% if loss_breakdown and loss_breakdown.ex_parte_score is not none %}
<section class="ex-parte-page">
  <h2>Ex parte verification capability</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Ex parte verification confirms an enrollee's hours or exemption against data the state already holds,
    so they never have to prove it themselves. It is the single biggest lever on the loss number above,
    and it has to be built before December 31, 2026. Here is how {{ state.name }} scores today.
  </p>

  {% set band = loss_breakdown.ex_parte_band %}
  {% set comp = loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.composite if loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores else loss_breakdown.ex_parte_score %}
  <div class="score-hero {% if band == 'high' %}score-hero--high{% elif band == 'low' %}score-hero--low{% endif %}">
    <div class="score-value">{{ comp }}<span class="score-of">/ 100</span></div>
    <div class="score-band">{{ loss_breakdown.ex_parte_band_label }}</div>
    <div class="score-bar"><div class="score-bar-fill" style="width: {{ comp }}%;"></div></div>
  </div>

  <table class="ex-parte-table">
    {% if loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores and loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.observed_ex_parte is not none %}
    <tr>
      <td class="exp-name">Observed ex parte</td>
      <td class="exp-score">{{ loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.observed_ex_parte }} / 100</td>
      <td class="exp-note">Realized auto-renewal rate, CMS unwinding data 2023–2026 (50% of composite)</td>
    </tr>
    {% endif %}
    {% if loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores and loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.data_sources is not none %}
    <tr>
      <td class="exp-name">Data sources</td>
      <td class="exp-score">{{ loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.data_sources }} / 100</td>
      <td class="exp-note">Work-requirement-specific feeds (35% of composite): wage 50%, frailty 30%, other 20%</td>
    </tr>
    {% endif %}
    {% if loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores and loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.core_capability is not none %}
    <tr class="exp-context">
      <td class="exp-name">Core capability</td>
      <td class="exp-score">{{ loss_breakdown.ex_parte_scores.core_capability }} / 100</td>
      <td class="exp-note">Vendor-tier plumbing, shown for context (not in the composite)</td>
    </tr>
    {% endif %}
  </table>

  <p>
    The 0–100 composite leads with {{ state.name }}'s observed ex parte rate: the share of Medicaid
    renewals it actually auto-completed during the 2023–2026 unwinding (CMS eligibility-processing data),
    a realized measure of whether it can clear an enrollee without making them act. That carries 50%.
    Data sources (the work-requirement-specific feeds income renewals didn't test: UI wage match,
    Medicaid claims and behavioral-health MCO, and the rest of the auto-match feeds) carry 35%, and
    documented prior Section 1115 work-requirement churn carries 15%.
    {% if loss_breakdown.system_vendor %}&nbsp;Eligibility system: <strong>{{ loss_breakdown.system_vendor }}</strong>.{% endif %}
  </p>
  <p class="section-note">
    Higher scores mean more enrollees can be verified against existing records without re-proving status.
    We don't publish a per-state predicted churn rate: the gap between "data source available" and "data
    source operationally integrated by the December 2026 deadline" is large and not yet observable. The
    score moves through operational work in 2026, not through anything that happens after the rule activates.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{# ============ Page 5 — What to do before December 31, 2026 ============ #}
<section class="action">
  <h2>What to do before December 31, 2026</h2>

  <ol class="checklist">
    {% for item in checklist %}
    <li>{{ item | safe }}</li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>

  {% if peers %}
    <h3>Peer states</h3>
    <p class="section-intro" style="margin-bottom: 4pt;">
      Comparable expansion populations, solving the same problem.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <th class="right">Subject pool</th>
          <th class="right">Burden index</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for p in peers %}
        <tr>
          <td class="county-name">{{ p.name }} ({{ p.abbr }})</td>
          <td class="right">{{ "{:,}".format(p.subject_count | round | int) }}</td>
          <td class="right">{{ "%+.1f"|format(p.burden_index) }}</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  {% endif %}

  <p style="margin-top: 10pt; margin-bottom: 5pt;">
    Every {{ state.name }} county, with subject count, projected loss, rate, and burden index, is in the CSV:
  </p>
  <a class="csv-button" href="{{ csv_url }}">Download {{ state.abbr }} county breakdown (CSV) →</a>

  <div class="methodology">
    <strong>Methodology, in brief.</strong>
    State counts are calibrated to CMS T-MSIS enrollment and the CBO national total; within-state detail and
    exemptions come from per-state ACS PUMS 2020-2024, SAMHSA, and BJS, with Section 1931 parents removed as
    covered outside expansion. The ex parte composite leads with the state's observed 2023-2026 renewal rate.
    Full methodology, sources, and limitations: <strong>gizmowarehouse.org/gizmo/medicaid-work-requirements</strong>.
    Corrections: <strong>joe@group17a.com</strong>. Built by 17A.
  </div>
</section>

{% endif %} {# end if state.expansion #}

</body>
</html>
